 - [x] Network Center (Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Connect, Cloud) **code complete 2026-08-29** — `darkos-network.py`. Wi-Fi/Bluetooth shell out to `nmcli`/`bluetoothctl` (the correct approach — NetworkManager/BlueZ own real hardware state, this doesn't reimplement that) and report the real failure rather than a fake device list. **Runtime-verified against two different real failure modes** in this sandbox: `nmcli` exits cleanly with "Could not connect: No such file or directory" (no NetworkManager running) and `bluetoothctl` actually aborts outright (SIGABRT, no D-Bus) — confirmed the app catches both without crashing itself and shows the real error text either way. Cloud tab is a stub (nothing exists to connect to before Phase 9).
 - [~] Connect (Network Center tab), **integrated 2026-09-06** — upstream `kdeconnect` owns discovery, TLS pairing, and plugins. The native tab reads typed D-Bus device status and offers confirmed pair/unpair, ring, and file sharing; notification/clipboard permissions remain in upstream settings. Thirteen hardware-free regressions and real GTK startup pass. Actual pairing and transfers still require a phone and a desktop session.
 - [~] Security Center (Vault, Privacy, Shield, Permissions, Encrypt) **partially complete 2026-08-30** — `darkos-security.py`, Vault and Encrypt operational, with remaining security scope tracked below: Vault and Encrypt use real, standard primitives (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256, 480,000 iterations + Fernet authenticated encryption from the `cryptography` library — not homemade crypto). **Runtime-verified end to end, including the failure paths that actually matter for a security tool, not just the happy path:** created a vault, added a real entry, locked it, unlocked with the correct password (succeeded) and a wrong password (correctly rejected, vault stayed locked) — confirmed via screenshots of both outcomes. Encrypted a real test file, confirmed the output is genuine ciphertext (checked the plaintext bytes do not appear anywhere in the encrypted file), decrypted with the correct passphrase and got back byte-identical content, then decrypted with a wrong passphrase and confirmed it's rejected with no corrupted output file ever written. Privacy tab is real saved toggles (staged for future enforcement, stated as such — DarkOS has no telemetry to begin with). Permissions tab mirrors Settings' own honest stub. **Update 2026-09-06:** Shield now has real on-demand scanning, described below; continuous protection and the Store gate are still open. Vault creation/locking, private atomic writes, failed-password handling, and output overwrite refusal now also have repeatable automated regressions.
-- [~] Shield, **on-demand scanning implemented 2026-09-06, quarantine added 2026-09-11, continuous protection added 2026-09-11** — cancellable ClamAV file/folder scans, bounded diagnostics, explicit failure/inspection-limit handling, and a user-triggered definitions updater. A detected result parses each hit out of ClamAV's real output (`parse_infected_files`) and moves it to quarantine (`quarantine.py` — move/restore/list/permanent-delete, one-click restore per architecture.md, never a silent delete, ownership-preserving for a privileged caller) instead of just reporting it — shared by both the on-demand scan and continuous protection via `scan_and_quarantine`, one code path, not two. Continuous protection (`continuous_watch.py` + `darkos-protect.py`) watches `~/Downloads` and `~/Desktop` and auto-scans anything dropped in. Built against fanotify first, per this line's original wording — switched to inotify after `fanotify_init`/`fanotify_mark` reported success but never once delivered a real event in testing, confirmed against a real file write with a blocking read, while the identical test against inotify worked immediately. inotify also doesn't need CAP_SYS_ADMIN, so this runs as the desktop user, not a separate root daemon. Launches via `hyprland.conf`'s `exec-once` (checked `start-hyprland` first: this session is tty1-autologin straight into Hyprland, no display manager, no systemd --user session ever starts — a `--user` unit, this line's earlier guess at how it'd be launched, would have silently never run), logs to `~/.local/share/darkos/protect.log`, and alerts through `notify-send` → mako on a detection rather than only writing a log line nobody's watching (`libnotify` added to `packages.x86_64` for this). 27 regressions pass across `ci/test-shield.py` and `ci/test-continuous-protection.py`, including a real end-to-end run: real file drop, real inotify event, real ClamAV detection, real quarantine, with a genuinely clean file in the same batch correctly left alone. **Quarantine review/restore UI added 2026-09-11**: a new Quarantine tab in Security Center lists real entries with Restore and Delete-permanently actions wired to the real `quarantine.py` calls (5 more regressions in `ci/test-security-ui.py`, 32 total) — building it surfaced the same GTK3 stock-chrome gap `scale`/`levelbar`/`progressbar` already had, this time on `Gtk.ListBox`/`row`, fixed the same way, and a real overflow bug (an unellipsized path pushed the action buttons off the visible row) caught by actually rendering it, not just compiling it. Not yet covered: removable-media mount points (inotify doesn't auto-discover new mounts under a watched parent — needs a udev/poll loop calling `rescan()`, not written), rkhunter/AIDE baselines, the Store installation scan gate, and a protection on/off toggle (darkos-protect.py currently just runs unconditionally once launched — no enable/disable mechanism exists yet).
+- [~] Shield — cancellable on-demand ClamAV scans (2026-09-06), quarantine + continuous protection + review UI (2026-09-11). Ten original scan regressions plus 27 more added today, 37 total across `ci/test-shield.py`, `ci/test-continuous-protection.py`, `ci/test-security-ui.py` — all passing, including two real end-to-end runs (on-demand and continuous) with genuine ClamAV detection and genuine quarantine, not mocked.
+  - **Quarantine** (`quarantine.py`): move/restore/list/permanent-delete, one-click restore per architecture.md, never silent-delete, ownership-preserving for a privileged caller. Shared by on-demand and continuous protection via `shield.scan_and_quarantine` — one code path, not two.
+  - **Continuous protection** (`continuous_watch.py` + `darkos-protect.py`): watches `~/Downloads` and `~/Desktop`. Built against fanotify first, per this line's original wording — switched to inotify after `fanotify_init`/`fanotify_mark` reported success but never once delivered a real event in testing (confirmed with a blocking read against a real file write). inotify also needs no CAP_SYS_ADMIN, so this runs as the desktop user, not a root daemon. Launches via `hyprland.conf`'s `exec-once` (checked `start-hyprland` first: tty1-autologin straight into Hyprland, no systemd --user session ever starts — a `--user` unit would have silently never run). Logs to `~/.local/share/darkos/protect.log`, alerts through `notify-send` → mako on a detection (`libnotify` added to `packages.x86_64`).
+  - **On/off toggle**: a switch in Security Center's Shield tab, backed by `user_settings.py`'s `shield_protection_enabled` (same settings.json every other preference already uses). `darkos-protect.py` checks it every ~30s via a `_reconcile` step and starts/stops the real watcher accordingly — takes effect within that window, not instantly, stated as the real trade-off it is rather than built to look instant and not be.
+  - **Review/restore UI**: a Quarantine tab in Security Center, real entries, Restore/Delete-permanently wired to the real calls. Surfaced two more real bugs by actually rendering it: the same GTK3 stock-chrome gap on `Gtk.ListBox`/`row` that `scale`/`levelbar`/`progressbar` already had (fixed the same way), and an unellipsized path overflow that pushed the action buttons off the visible row (fixed, and now has a regression test specifically because it happened).
+  - **Not yet covered**: removable-media mount points (inotify doesn't auto-discover new mounts under a watched parent — needs a udev/poll loop calling `rescan()`), rkhunter/AIDE baselines, the Store installation scan gate.
 - [ ] Network transparency dashboard (per-app, plain-language) — reuses PHANTOM's monitoring core. **Blocked on input, not effort:** PHANTOM's source isn't in this repo or this conversation — reusing its actual monitoring core means either Hamza sharing that code, or reimplementing something PHANTOM-*like* from scratch, which risks quietly diverging from what "reuse" was meant to mean here.
 - [x] Backup / Recovery **code complete 2026-08-30** — `darkos-backup.py`. Plain file-based backup (Python `tarfile`, not Btrfs snapshots — this sandbox's filesystem is ext2/ext3 so a snapshot approach couldn't be verified here regardless, and the spec didn't call for one specifically). A manifest at `~/.local/share/darkos/backups/manifest.json` tracks what was backed up, when, and how big it was. **Runtime-verified end to end:** backed up a real test folder through the actual UI, confirmed the resulting `.tar.gz` has correct internal paths (`tar -tzf`), then restored it and diffed the restored file against the original — byte-identical. `tarfile.extractall(..., filter="data")` (PEP 706) same as File Explorer's archive handling, guarding against path-traversal from an archive of unknown origin.
 - [x] Dashboard (Performance, Overlay); [x] Mission/Spaces — **both done 2026-08-30.** `darkos-dashboard.py`: live CPU% (computed from two `/proc/stat` samples a tick apart, the standard technique), memory, disk, and top-processes-by-memory, refreshing every 2 seconds. **Runtime-verified with an actual load test** — ran a real CPU-bound busy-loop in the background and confirmed the dashboard's CPU reading actually moved from 0% to 100% between ticks, and that the busy-loop's own process appeared in the live top-processes list. `darkos-mission.py`: real `hyprctl -j workspaces` / `hyprctl -j clients` calls, grouped by workspace, with real `hyprctl dispatch` actions to switch workspace or focus a window. **Correction from earlier in this same session:** this was first bucketed with Shield as "can't verify here" — that was too pessimistic. Shield's correctness is fundamentally unverifiable without a real scan engine; this is a data-display problem against hyprctl's documented, stable JSON schema, which is a fundamentally different and much more tractable category — same shape as Network Center's nmcli/bluetoothctl calls. Verified two separate things, both for real: (1) the graceful-failure path, which is genuinely real in this sandbox (`hyprctl is not installed`, confirmed via screenshot), and (2) the parsing/rendering logic, via a schema-accurate fake `hyprctl` script on `PATH` returning realistic workspace/client JSON (documented format, not invented) — confirmed correct grouping including an empty-workspace case, correct unicode title decoding, and confirmed the dispatch buttons don't crash the app when clicked. What's still unverified is purely cosmetic/compositor-specific (does it look right against real windows) — the logic itself has been checked both ways it can be.
